Intuition
Imagine physically overlaying one tree on top of the other, like stacking two transparent sheets with tree diagrams drawn on them. Wherever both sheets have a node at the same position, the numbers add together. Wherever only one sheet has a node, that node just shows through as-is. Wherever neither has a node, that spot stays blank.

This "overlay" framing tells you immediately that you're doing a simultaneous traversal of both trees at once, position by position — not two separate traversals. At every position, you only have four possible situations: both nodes present, only one present (two variants), or neither present. Once you frame the problem that way, the recursion almost writes itself.

Approach
Use recursion (DFS), walking both trees at the same time, node by node:

Base case — both missing: if the current position is None in both trees, there's nothing to merge here — return None.
Base case — only one tree has a node: if one side is None, there's no merging math to do — just return the other tree's node (and everything below it) unchanged.
Recursive case — both have a node: create a new node whose value is the sum of both current values. Then recursively merge the left children together, and recursively merge the right children together, attaching the results.

Because recursion naturally stops the instant one side runs out of nodes (step 2), you never have to explicitly check "are we out of bounds" — running out of a tree is the base case.

Complexity
Time: O(min(n1, n2)) — where n1, n2 are node counts of the two trees. Recursion only continues where both trees still have nodes; the moment one side hits None, that branch terminates immediately (step 2 above), so you never traverse further into the larger tree than the smaller tree allows.
Space: O(min(h1, h2)) — for the recursion call stack, where h1, h2 are the heights of the two trees. Same reasoning: recursion depth is bounded by whichever tree runs out first.

Code
# Definition for a binary tree node.
# class TreeNode:
#     def __init__(self, val=0, left=None, right=None):
#         self.val = val
#         self.left = left
#         self.right = right
class Solution:
    def mergeTrees(self, root1: Optional[TreeNode], root2: Optional[TreeNode]) -> Optional[TreeNode]:
        if root1 is None and root2 is None:
            return None
        if root1 is None:
            return root2
        if root2 is None:
            return root1
        mergedNode = TreeNode(root1.val + root2.val)
        mergedNode.left = self.mergeTrees(root1.left,root2.left)
        mergedNode.right = self.mergeTrees(root1.right,root2.right)
        return mergedNode
Edge cases worth mentioning in an interview
Both trees completely empty → returns None (empty merged tree).
One tree completely empty → returns the other tree exactly, node objects reused (not deep-copied — worth flagging if the interviewer cares about mutating input).
Trees of very different shapes/sizes → handled naturally since each side's None just short-circuits that branch.
